# Binary MCP Configuration
# Copy this file to .env and fill in your values
# Environment variables override .env file values

# VIRUSTOTAL
# Get your API key from: https://www.virustotal.com/gui/my-apikey
VT_API_KEY=your_virustotal_api_key_here

# GHIDRA
# Path to Ghidra installation (auto-detected if not set)
# GHIDRA_HOME=/opt/ghidra

# Analysis timeout in seconds (default: 1800)
# GHIDRA_TIMEOUT=1800

# JVM max heap for the Ghidra subprocess, in MB (default: 4096)
# Bump to 6144-8192 for very large binaries -- a full-depth run holds every
# function AND every pseudocode body in memory before serialising, which is
# where a multi-MB DLL with 20K+ functions runs out of heap.
# Applied via _JAVA_OPTIONS, which the JVM parses after the command line, so
# it overrides the -Xmx Ghidra's own launcher passes. The exception: an -Xmx
# ALREADY present in _JAVA_OPTIONS wins and is left alone, so a global
# "-Xmx2g" in your environment silently caps Ghidra regardless of the value
# below. Check `echo $_JAVA_OPTIONS` first if Ghidra OOMs despite raising this.
# GHIDRA_MAX_HEAP_MB=4096

# X64DBG
# Where the Obsidian plugin's HTTP listener is. A tunnel whose local end is
# 127.0.0.1 needs nothing below it. A non-loopback host requires
# BINARY_MCP_REMOTE_ALLOW, X64DBG_TLS_CA and OBSIDIAN_AUTH_TOKEN. The plugin's
# own listener serves TLS when its obsidian.ini names a certificate; a TLS
# terminator in front of it works the same from here. See
# docs/remote-access.md.
# X64DBG_HOST=127.0.0.1
# X64DBG_PORT=8765

# Command timeout in seconds (default: 30)
# X64DBG_TIMEOUT=30

# CA that signs the debugger host's certificate. Setting it selects https.
# X64DBG_TLS_CA=
# Client certificate to present to the debugger host (mutual TLS).
# X64DBG_TLS_CLIENT_CERT=
# X64DBG_TLS_CLIENT_KEY=

# Hosts to reach directly, bypassing any configured proxy. Needed when
# HTTPS_PROXY is set in this server's environment: requests routes the
# debugger connection through it and the symptom is a reset or a timeout
# against a host that is plainly up. Add the debugger host here.
#
# Unlike the settings above this is not read by this project at all -- urllib
# resolves it per request -- so it only works because config.py publishes it
# into the real environment. It is declared in _PASSTHROUGH_ENV_KEYS for that
# purpose; an undeclared name in this file is honoured in-process but never
# exported, so a library that reads the environment would not see it.
# no_proxy=10.10.40.25

# Bearer token for the plugin's API. Optional on loopback (the bridge reads the
# plugin's %TEMP% token file); required for a non-loopback host, where that
# file is on the other machine.
# OBSIDIAN_AUTH_TOKEN=

# STORAGE
# Base directory for all on-disk state: analysis cache, Ghidra projects,
# saved sessions, job records, per-engine error logs.
# BINARY_CACHE_DIR=/tmp/binary-mcp/cache

# Session storage directory (default: ~/.binary_mcp_sessions)
# BINARY_MCP_SESSION_DIR=/tmp/binary-mcp/sessions

# TRANSPORT
# stdio (default) means the client spawns this server itself. Set http to let a
# client on another host connect. A non-loopback bind additionally requires
# BINARY_MCP_REMOTE_ALLOW, a TLS cert/key, and an explicit token.
# See docs/remote-access.md.
# BINARY_MCP_TRANSPORT=stdio
# BINARY_MCP_HTTP_HOST=127.0.0.1
# BINARY_MCP_HTTP_PORT=8770
# BINARY_MCP_HTTP_PATH=/mcp
# BINARY_MCP_HTTP_TOKEN=
# BINARY_MCP_HTTP_ALLOWED_HOSTS=
# BINARY_MCP_REMOTE_ALLOW=
# BINARY_MCP_REMOTE_TLS_CERT=
# BINARY_MCP_REMOTE_TLS_KEY=
# BINARY_MCP_REMOTE_TLS_CA=
# BINARY_MCP_REMOTE_CLIENT_ALLOWLIST=

# LOGGING
# Log level: DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR
# BINARY_MCP_LOG_LEVEL=INFO
